{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":29839319,"ns":0,"title":"Hexacyclinol","revisions":[{"revid":1292433851,"parentid":1229892426,"minor":true,"timestamp":"2025-05-26T22:46:51Z","size":7465,"sha1":"1c1493d5ad3817547f3589d7eee9a5fbd2169a86","slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","content":"{{chembox\n| Watchedfields = changed\n| verifiedrevid = 421606671\n| ImageFile = Hexacyclinol-2D-skeletal.png\n| ImageCaption = '''(1)''' The structure for hexacyclinol proposed by Rychnovsky and synthesized by Porco, Jr.\n| ImageSize = \n| ImageFile1 = Hexacyclinol-from-xtal-2010-3D-balls-B.png\n| IUPACName = (2a''S'',3''R'',5a''R'',6a''S'',7''R'',8''R'',8a''S'',10''R'')-7-hydroxy-3-(1-methoxy-1-methylethyl)-10-(2-methylprop-1-en-1-yl)-1a,5a,6a,7,7a,7b,8,8a-octahydro-2''H''-8,2a-(epoxymethano)phenanthro[2,3-b:6,7-b']bisoxirene-2,5(3''H'')-dione\n| OtherNames = \n|Section1={{Chembox Identifiers\n| Abbreviations = \n| CASNo = 903574-41-4\n| CASNo_Ref = {{Cascite|changed|PubChem}}\n| UNII = UG4YWN1CPX\n| EINECS = \n| PubChem = 11975375\n| ChemSpiderID_Ref = {{chemspidercite|correct|chemspider}}\n| ChemSpiderID = 10148727\n| SMILES = O=C5[C@]34[C@H](\\C=C2\\C(=O)[C@H]1O[C@H]1[C@H](O)[C@H]2[C@@H]3[C@@H](O[C@@H]4\\C=C(/C)C)[C@@H]6O[C@H]56)C(OC)(C)C\n| StdInChI_Ref = {{stdinchicite|correct|chemspider}}\n| StdInChI = 1S/C23H28O7/c1-8(2)6-11-23-10(22(3,4)27-5)7-9-12(15(25)18-17(29-18)14(9)24)13(23)16(28-11)19-20(30-19)21(23)26/h6-7,10-13,15-20,25H,1-5H3/t10-,11-,12-,13-,15-,16-,17-,18+,19+,20+,23+/m1/s1\n| StdInChIKey_Ref = {{stdinchicite|correct|chemspider}}\n| StdInChIKey = PFZFRWWDGXFULQ-NHFKQXEKSA-N\n| RTECS =\n| MeSHName = C469535\n| ChEBI = \n| KEGG_Ref = {{keggcite|correct|kegg}}\n| KEGG = \n}}\n|Section2={{Chembox Properties\n| C=23 | H=28 | O=7\n| Appearance =\n| Density = \n| MeltingPt = \n| MeltingPt_notes =\n| BoilingPt = \n| BoilingPt_notes = \n| Solubility = \n| SolubleOther = \n| Solvent = \n| pKa = \n| pKb = \n  }}\n}}\n\n'''Hexacyclinol''' is a [[natural product|natural metabolite]] of a [[fungus]], ''[[Lentinus strigosus|Panus rudis]]''. Significant controversy surrounded its proposed structure until its [[total synthesis]] by John Porco, Jr. in 2006.\n\n==Controversy over structure==\n[[File:Hexacyclinol-grafe-oriented.png|left|thumb|'''(2)''' The structure of hexacyclinol proposed by Gräfe and purportedly synthesized by La Clair.]]\nNatural products chemist Udo Gräfe collected a sample of ''P. rudis'' HKI 0254 from a dead log in [[Siberia]] from which hexacyclinol was isolated. His group's 2002 paper showed that the compound behaved as an antiproliferative drug against [[cancer]] cell lines and proposed a structure '''(2)''' for the compound.<ref name=\"Grafe\">{{cite journal |author= Brigitte Schlegel |author2=Albert Härtl |author3=Hans-Martin Dahse |author4=Friedrich A. Gollmick |author5=Udo Gräfe |author6=Heinrich Dörfelt |author7=Barbara Kappes |year=2002 |title=Hexacyclinol, a New Antiproliferative Metabolite of ''Panus rudis'' HKI 0254 |journal=The Journal of Antibiotics  |volume=55 |issue=9 |pages=814–817 |pmid=12458771 |doi= 10.7164/antibiotics.55.814 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n\nAn initial total synthesis was published by James J. La Clair in 2006, purporting a synthesis of Gräfe's proposed structure based on [[Proton NMR|<sup>1</sup>H nuclear magnetic resonance]] ([[nuclear magnetic resonance|NMR]]) spectra.<ref name=\"LaClair\">{{cite journal |last1= La Clair |first1 = James J. |year=2006 |title=Total Syntheses of Hexacyclinol, 5-''epi''-Hexacyclinol, and Desoxohexacyclinol Unveil an Antimalarial Prodrug Motif |journal=Angewandte Chemie International Edition  |volume=45 |issue=17 |pages=2769–2773 |doi=10.1002/anie.200504033 |pmid = 16470558 |doi-access= }}{{Retracted|doi=10.1002/anie.201206869|pmid=23297422|http://retractionwatch.com/2012/11/14/chemistry-journal-and-author-retract-paper-dogged-by-questions-since-its-publication-in-2006/ ''Retraction Watch''|intentional=yes}}</ref> \nNatural products chemist Scott D. Rychnovsky simulated the [[carbon NMR|<sup>13</sup>C nuclear magnetic resonance]] spectrum of the structure proposed by Gräfe and found that it did not correspond to the spectrum of the structure allegedly synthesized by La Clair. Rychnovsky proposed a different structure '''(1)''' based on panepophenanthrin, another molecule isolated from a different strain of ''P. rudis''.<ref name=\"Rych\">{{cite journal |author= Scott D. Rychnovsky |year=2006 |title=Predicting NMR Spectra by Computational Methods: Structure Revision of Hexacyclinol |journal=Organic Letters |volume=8 |issue=13 |pages=2895–2898 |doi=10.1021/ol0611346 |pmid= 16774284 |url=https://figshare.com/articles/Predicting_NMR_Spectra_by_Computational_Methods_Structure_Revision_of_Hexacyclinol/3074974 |url-access=subscription }}</ref> The scientific community then began criticizing La Clair's work, claiming that his work was sloppy or that he fabricated data.<ref name=\"C&EN\">{{cite web |url=http://pubs.acs.org/cen/news/84/i31/8431notw1.html |title= Chemical & Engineering News: Latest News - Hexacyclinol Debate Heats Up |author=Bethany Halford |date=28 July 2006 |website= |publisher=Chemical & Engineering News |access-date=2 December 2010}}</ref>  La Clair's publication of his purported synthesis was retracted in 2012, citing a lack of validation of its claims.<ref>{{Cite journal | title = Retraction: Total Syntheses of Hexacyclinol, 5-epi-Hexacyclinol, and Desoxohexacyclinol Unveil an Antimalarial Prodrug Motif | author = James J. La Clair | volume = 51 | issue = 47 | pages = 11661 | year = 2012 | doi = 10.1002/anie.201206869 | journal=Angewandte Chemie International Edition| pmid = 23297422 | doi-access = free }}</ref>\n\nIn 2006, a group led by John Porco, Jr. synthesized Rychnovsky's proposed structure. They showed that the <sup>1</sup>H- and <sup>13</sup>C-NMR spectra matched that of the compound isolated by Gräfe, confirming  Rychnovsky's structure.<ref name=\"Porco\">{{cite journal |author= John A. Porco Jr. |author2=Shun Su |author3=Xiaoguang Lei |author4=Sujata Bardhan |author5=Scott D. Rychnovsky  |year=2006 |title=Total Synthesis and Structure Assignment of (+)-Hexacyclinol |journal=Angewandte Chemie International Edition |volume=45 |issue=35 |pages=5790–5792 |doi=10.1002/anie.200602854 |pmid=16871643}}</ref> La Clair claimed that since the two structures were [[Structural isomer|isomer]]s, it is possible that they would have similar <sup>1</sup>H-NMR spectra.<ref name=\"C&EN\"/> However, a later paper by Saielli and Bagno claims that there would be significant differences in the <sup>1</sup>H- and <sup>13</sup>C-NMR spectra of compounds '''(1)''' and '''(2)'''.<ref name=\"SB\">{{cite journal |author= Giacomo Saielli |author2=Alessandro Bagno  |year=2009 |title=Can Two Molecules Have the Same NMR Spectrum? Hexacyclinol Revisited |journal=Organic Letters |volume=11 |issue=6 |pages=1409–1412\n|doi=10.1021/ol900164a |pmid= 19215104 }}</ref>\n\nThe controversy was covered extensively by a number of science [[blog]]s.<ref name=\"CENblog\">{{cite web |url=https://cen.acs.org/articles/84/i31/Hexacyclinol-Debate-Heats.html |title= Hexacyclinol-The Data Debate / CENtral Science |author=Carmen Drahl and Bethany Halford |date=26 February 2009 |website= |publisher=Chemical & Engineering News blog |access-date=2 December 2010}}</ref>\n\nIn response to the controversy, [[Nobel Prize in Chemistry|Nobel Prize]]-winning synthetic chemist [[E.J. Corey]] remarked, \"Occasionally, blatantly wrong science is published, and to the credit of synthetic chemistry, the corrections usually come quickly and cleanly.\"<ref name=\"C&EN\"/>\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:Total synthesis]]\n[[Category:Polyketides]]\n[[Category:Epoxides]]\n[[Category:Heterocyclic compounds with 6 rings]]\n[[Category:Ketones]]"}}}],"contentmodel":"wikitext","pagelanguage":"en","pagelanguagehtmlcode":"en","pagelanguagedir":"ltr","touched":"2026-11-17T19:45:45Z","lastrevid":1292433851,"length":7465}]}}